    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Comparison of elastically modeled present day maximum principal compression directions (S3) across the Elk Hills Oil Field, CA with well bore breakout azimuths indicates that the mechanical model captures, to a first order, the stress directions currently operative within the field. We first forward model for the stress field perturbations induced by current day slip along the relatively active faults beneath Elk Hills. We then determine the corresponding S3 directions and note how local directional variations correlate with horizontal principal compressive stress (SH) directions interpreted within proximal wellbores. Through this investigation, we identify where additional parameters should be considered within the elastic models, as well as where the model appears to be well-calibrated and may yield a predicted fracture pattern that is representative of the existing fracture pattern.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
